.playwright/

# env — app.prod.env and app.dev.env hold op:// references only; any other .env* file may hold real values.
.env
.env.*

# scratch from the sourcing-media skill's brief scan. It records the framework,
# the asset dir and the candidate slots it detected — an input to
# a media plan, not a record worth keeping, and regenerated
# by re-running the brief.
.media-work/

# reference-site captures from `pnpm reference:capture`; local only, too large for git
.reference/

# playwright run artifacts
test-results/
playwright-report/

# terraform — never commit state or real variable values
**/.terraform/*
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
crash.log
override.tf
override_*.tf
!terraform.tfvars.example
infra/.*.log
infra/.bootstrap.zip

# Saved plan files. A plan holds the full planned state, which means every
# generated password and every SSM parameter value the apply would write, in
# cleartext — the same exposure as the state file, from a file whose name looks
# like a scratch artefact. `terraform plan -out=tfplan` produces one in the
# working directory by default, so this pattern is what stops an ordinary
# `git add infra/` from committing it.
tfplan
tfplan-*
*.tfplan

# State is local and NEVER committed. It holds the generated Postgres password
# and PAYLOAD_SECRET in plaintext. Losing it means orphaned AWS resources that
# terraform destroy can no longer see — back up infra/terraform.tfstate
# somewhere private if the stack is real.
#
# The path above was infra/envs/*/terraform.tfstate, and that directory does not
# exist: the envs/dev and envs/prod roots were collapsed into a single flat
# infra/ root, a rename that is staged in this working tree. An instruction to
# back up a path that cannot exist reads as satisfied by anyone who globs it and
# finds nothing. CLAUDE.md's "Never touch" list carries the same stale path for
# both the state and the tfvars.

# technomancy
technomancy_artifacts/
SPEC_*.md

# project-local postgres cluster (db:server:*)
.pgdata/

